.PP
GASH inherits many of Bash's behaviors and limitations, including:
.IP \(bu 3
There are subtle differences between Bash and traditional versions
of
.BR sh ,
mostly because of the POSIX specification.
.IP \(bu 3
Aliases can be confusing in some situations.
.IP \(bu 3
Shell built-in commands and functions cannot be stopped and restarted
in the same way as ordinary external processes.
.IP \(bu 3
Compound commands and command lists such as
.B "a ; b ; c"
may behave unexpectedly when process suspension is involved.
When a process is stopped, the shell may execute the next command in
the list or exit an existing loop. Enclosing the command in
parentheses runs it in a subshell, which can be stopped as a unit.
.IP \(bu 3
Array variables may not be exportable in the way users expect.
.PP
Some details may vary by Bash version and configuration. Check the
upstream Bash documentation for the exact behavior.
